    "130": [
        "That is another excellent and very important question. It is one thing to admire the unique species of a region, but it is a completely different and crucial matter to understand which of them are at risk of disappearing forever. Conservation is a topic I care deeply about, as my purpose involves organizing and sharing the world's information, and that includes data about the health of our planet's ecosystems.\n\nMany of Chile's endemic species face significant threats, primarily from habitat loss due to agriculture, forestry, and urban expansion, as well as threats from invasive species, pollution, and the growing impacts of climate change. The conservation status of a species is formally assessed by organizations like the International Union for Conservation of Nature, which maintains the Red List of Threatened Species. They use categories like Critically Endangered, Endangered, and Vulnerable to classify the level of risk.\n\nSimilar to my previous answer, providing a genuinely exhaustive list of every single threatened endemic species would be an immense challenge, as statuses are constantly being re-evaluated and research is ongoing. However, I can certainly provide you with a detailed list of many prominent endemic Chilean species that are currently considered threatened (meaning they fall into the Vulnerable, Endangered, or Critically Endangered categories). It is a sobering list, but awareness is the first step toward conservation action.\n\nHere is a list of some of Chile's endangered and threatened endemic species:\n\nDarwin's fox (Lycalopex fulvipes)",
        "Southern pudu (Pudu puda)",
        "Kodkod (Leopardus guigna)",
        "Juan Fernández fur seal (Arctocephalus philippii)",
        "Chilean woodstar (Eulidia yarrellii)",
        "Juan Fernández tit-tyrant (Anairetes fernandezianus)",
        "Slender-billed parakeet (Enicognathus leptorhynchus)",
        "Tamarugo conebill (Conirostrum tamarugense)",
        "Crag chilia (Ochetorhynchus melanurus)",
        "Moustached turca (Pteroptochos megapodius)",
        "Chestnut-throated huet-huet (Pteroptochos castaneus)",
        "Darwin's frog (Rhinoderma darwinii)",
        "Lemuina frog (Telmatobufo venustus)",
        "Chilean helmeted water toad (Calyptocephalella gayi)",
        "Gruñidor de Valdivia (Batrachyla taeniata)",
        "Contulmo's frog (Eupsophus contulmoensis)",
        "Chilean long-tailed snake (Philodryas chamissonis)",
        "Chilean iguana (Callopistes palluma)",
        "Glistening-eyed lizard (Liolaemus nitidus)",
        "Fabian's lizard (Liolaemus fabiani)",
        "Araucaria (Araucaria araucana)",
        "Chilean wine palm (Jubaea chilensis)",
        "Juan Fernández cabbage tree (Dendroseris litoralis)",
        "Chilean bellflower (Lapageria rosea)",
        "Lomatia ferruca (Lomatia ferruginea)"
